60,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Candidate A, Home Follow-Up, has no tariff. It will be a pure cost.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Shion,,Candidate A relies on home visits. The shared round data shows high failure rates.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,"Candidate C, Perinatal Support, uses the midwifery slack. That is a good fit.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"Candidate B, Day Rehab, needs dedicated space. The rooms are already full.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,Candidate C is jointly run. The board minutes show that model fails.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, that is a strong point. Joint management is a risk for C.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Midori, the midwifery slack is real, but the joint aspect is a problem.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, the pediatric side is stable. The midwifery side has the capacity.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Jonathan, Candidate D, Chronic Care, uses existing booking slots. It fits.","{""words"":11,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,Candidate D involves outside surgeries. The data agreements take fourteen months.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,Candidate D has no new rooms. It fits the budget and capacity constraints.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Shion,,Candidate D consolidates appointments. It reduces the need for new infrastructure.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Midori,,"Candidate C is attractive, but the joint management risk is too high.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Kenji,,Candidate D uses the booking system effectively. It does not add to the queue.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"Candidate D requires data agreements. That is a delay, but manageable.","{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I am leaning towards D. It is the most sustainable financially.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Shion,,I agree. D has the best chance of lasting beyond the first year.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Midori,,"I am concerned about C, but D seems safer for the staffing.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Kenji,,D fits the physical constraints. It is the most practical choice.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shizue,,D avoids the joint management trap. It is the most stable option.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
